2 and 3. Information as to denominations was not maintained by Battalion formation.
4.	Neither of the Commanding Officers of the Battalions mentioned above were Roman Catholics.

Mr. Speaker, when the House rose at six o'clock I had just completed reading a list of military electors who had given their answers to the questions on the envelope, and had declared that they were United States citizens, but who nevertheless voted in a number of constituencies throughout the Dominion. That was to prove charge No. 26 that a large number of citizens of the United States had voted in the following Canadian constituencies: Peel, Ontario; Digby and Annapolis, N.S.; St. Ann's, Quebec; Prince Edward, Ontario; North Essex, and Bruce North, Ontario; Edmonton East, Alberta; Cape Breton South and Richmond, N.S.;

St. Lawrence and St. George, Montreal; Ottawa; Brome, Quebec; Renfrew, Ontario; Laurie,r and Outremont, and Wright, Quebec; Gape Breton North and Victoria, and Pictou, Nova Scotia; Westmorland, New Brunswick; Edmonton W., Alberta; Cumberland, and Yarmouth, Nova Scotia; and Leeds, Ontario.
I want to read another list on the same matter of citizens of the United States, nonresident voters, who answered No. 5 on the ballot envelope in the negative. Therei were twenty on the list in question, all of whom voted in the Yukon electoral district. Their names are:
